 ## Background
-Warp-based methods were introduced as a class of dynamic scene representations that condition the representation with a time component, modeling geometry and appearance as a continuous function of both space and time [S1]. They differ from space-time representations by explicitly using a deformation network rather than extending the query space with time [S1].
+Warp-based methods were introduced as a class of space-time representations that condition the representation with a time component [S1]. They model geometry and appearance as a continuous function of both space and time [S1]. Key references include [PSB* 21, PSH* 21, TTG* 21, LDZY24, WYF* 24, YGZ* 24] for warping-based methods and [GSD* 23] for forward warp approaches [S1].
 
